.TH NIXALARM 5 "2026-07-24" "nixalarm 0.2.0" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
nixalarm \- configuration file for nixalarm
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nixalarm
reads a small TOML-like configuration file. If no file exists, built-in defaults
are written to disk and then loaded.
.PP
The default path is:
.PP
.RS
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/nixalarm/config.toml
.RE
.PP
If
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset, the fallback path is:
.PP
.RS
.I $HOME/.config/nixalarm/config.toml
.RE
.PP
The parser supports simple
.I key = value
assignments, quoted strings, integers, floating point numbers, booleans, and
sections. It is intentionally small and does not implement the full TOML
language.
.SH TOP-LEVEL KEYS
.TP
.B alarm_source = "generated"
Default alarm source name.
.TP
.B alarms = ["07:30"]
List of default alarm times used when
.BR nixalarm (1)
is launched without a command line time, such as from a desktop application
menu. Times without
.B AM
or
.B PM
are parsed as 24-hour time. Times with
.B AM
or
.B PM
are parsed as 12-hour time. Use an empty list for clock-only no-argument
launches.
.TP
.B snooze_minutes = 10
Minutes added when Space is tapped while the alarm is ringing.
.TP
.B hold_to_stop_seconds = 10
Seconds Space must be held continuously while ringing to dismiss the alarm.
.TP
.B volume = 0.9
Playback volume from
.B 0.0
to
.BR 1.0 .
.TP
.B source_start_timeout_seconds = 8
FFmpeg read timeout, in seconds, for file and network sources.
.TP
.B fallback_source = "generated"
Source to try when the selected source fails.
.TP
.B use_24_hour = false
Use a 24-hour clock display. The default
.B false
uses 12-hour display. Alarm times on the command line still use 24-hour
.IR HH:MM .
.TP
.B latitude = 0.0
Observer latitude in signed degrees, positive north, clamped to
.RB [ -90 ,
.BR 90 ].
Used only by the
.BR sundial ,
.B moondial
and
.B auto_dial
themes, whose accuracy depends on it; the default 0.0 is not a sentinel but a
real place (off the west coast of Africa).
.TP
.B longitude = 0.0
Observer longitude in signed degrees, positive east, clamped to
.RB [ -180 ,
.BR 180 ].
Used only by the
.BR sundial ,
.B moondial
and
.B auto_dial
themes.
.SH WINDOW SECTION
.SS [window]
.TP
.B width = 800
Initial window width.
.TP
.B height = 360
Initial window height.
.TP
.B fullscreen = false
Start in fullscreen desktop mode.
.TP
.B always_on_top = false
Request an always-on-top window.
.TP
.B flash_hz = 2.0
Flash rate, in hertz, while the alarm is ringing. Set to
.B 0
to disable flashing.
.SH STYLE SECTION
.SS [style]
.TP
.B theme = "terminal_glow"
Select the built-in visual theme. A theme sets both the clock rendering style
and its colors. Set this before any color overrides.
.PP
Supported themes:
.RS
.TP
.B terminal_glow
Seven-segment digits. Dark green background, bright green digits, dim
inactive segments, and glow.
.TP
.B sinnoh_green
Seven-segment digits. Light green pixel-clock style with dark green block
digits and no glow.
.TP
.B nixie
Photoreal nixie-tube digits. Does not use the color overrides below.
.TP
.B analog
SVG-rendered analog dial based on the public-domain Wikimedia Commons animated
analog clock, with the original script/branding removed and the hand motion
driven by C++. A small alarm marker appears when an alarm or snooze is pending
and pulses while ringing.
.TP
.B sundial
A horizontal sundial, ray-traced in OpenGL, telling the time by the shadow the
sun actually casts at the configured
.B latitude
and
.BR longitude .
The plate tilt and hour-line spacing are optimized for that latitude at
startup. Requires OpenGL 3.3. Does not use the color overrides below apart
from
.BR background ,
which fills the area around the dial. Dark once the sun can no longer be read.
.TP
.B moondial
The same instrument built for the Moon instead, and dark by day. Same
requirements and color behavior as
.BR sundial .
.TP
.B auto_dial
Both dials as one clock: whichever can currently be read, crossfading from one
to the other at the changeover. The changeover follows readability rather than
sunset -- a dial stops telling the time once the gnomon's shadow slides off the
edge of the plate, some while before the body sets -- and the moon dial takes
over only if the Moon is up and more than roughly a quarter lit. When neither
dial can be read the face holds the one it was showing and goes dark. Same
requirements and color behavior as
.BR sundial .
.RE
.TP
.B background = "#07180b"
Window background color. Ignored by the
.B nixie
theme.
.TP
.B segment_on = "#6cff57"
Active segment color. Ignored by the
.B nixie
theme.
.TP
.B segment_off = "#123319"
Inactive segment color. Ignored by the
.B nixie
theme.
.TP
.B glow = true
Draw a soft glow behind active segments. Ignored by the
.B nixie
theme.
.TP
.B show_seconds = false
Show seconds in the clock display. For the
.B analog
theme, this draws or hides only the seconds hand; the
.B analog
theme enables it by default.
.TP
.B roman_numerals = false
For the
.B analog
theme, use the Roman-numeral SVG face instead of the default Arabic-numeral
SVG face.
.TP
.B analog_midnight_label = "24"
Reserved for non-SVG 24-hour analog faces; ignored by the current
.B analog
theme, which follows its source SVG's 12-hour Arabic dial.
.SH SOURCE SECTIONS
.SS [sources.NAME]
Each source section adds or overrides an audio source named
.IR NAME .
.PP
Supported source types are
.BR generated ,
.BR file ,
.BR internet ,
.BR midi ,
and
.BR sdr_weatherband .
.SS Generated source
.EX
[sources.beep]
type = "generated"
.EE
.SS Local file source
.EX
[sources.local_song]
type = "file"
path = "/home/user/Music/alarm.flac"
.EE
.PP
Files are decoded through FFmpeg. Format support depends on the system FFmpeg
build.
.SS Internet source
.EX
[sources.weather_stream]
type = "internet"
url = "https://example.org/your-stream"
.EE
.PP
Network streams are decoded through FFmpeg and fall back to
.B fallback_source
when startup or playback fails.
.SS MIDI source
.EX
[sources.midi_alarm]
type = "midi"
path = "/home/user/Music/alarm.mid"
soundfont = "/usr/share/soundfonts/default.sf2"
.EE
.PP
MIDI files are rendered through FluidSynth. If
.B soundfont
is omitted,
.B nixalarm
tries a few common system SoundFont paths. If no SoundFont can be found, the
source fails and the configured fallback source is used.
.SS RTL-SDR weather-band source
.EX
[sources.weather_sdr]
type = "sdr_weatherband"
frequency_mhz = 162.500
device_index = 0
gain = "auto"
.EE
.PP
SDR sources require RTL-SDR hardware and the
.BR rtl_fm (1)
program at runtime.
.SH COMPLETE EXAMPLE
.EX
alarm_source = "generated"
alarms = ["07:30", "5:30 PM"]
snooze_minutes = 10
hold_to_stop_seconds = 10
volume = 0.9
source_start_timeout_seconds = 8
fallback_source = "generated"
use_24_hour = false
latitude = 0.0
longitude = 0.0

[window]
width = 800
height = 360
fullscreen = false
always_on_top = false
flash_hz = 2.0

[style]
theme = "sinnoh_green"
show_seconds = false
roman_numerals = false
analog_midnight_label = "24"

[sources.local_song]
type = "file"
path = "/home/user/Music/alarm.flac"

[sources.midi_alarm]
type = "midi"
path = "/home/user/Music/alarm.mid"
soundfont = "/usr/share/soundfonts/default.sf2"

[sources.weather_stream]
type = "internet"
url = "https://example.org/your-stream"

[sources.weatherband]
type = "sdr_weatherband"
frequency_mhz = 162.500
device_index = 0
gain = "auto"
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR nixalarm (1),
.BR rtl_fm (1),
.BR ffmpeg (1)
